","a) **Series Structure**:  
   
   In a serial system, all components must function for the system to work.  
   
   Let \( R_i(t) \) be the return rate (probability of success) of the \( i \)-th component, where \( i = 1, 2, \ldots, n \).  
   
   The system response rate \( R_S(t) \) is the product of the response rates of all components:  
   
   \[
   R_S(t) = R_1(t) \cdot R_2(t) \cdot \ldots \cdot R_n(t) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} R_i(t)
   \]  
   
   The system failure rate \( F_S(t) \) is:  
   
   \[
   F_S(t) = 1 - R_S(t) = 1 - \prod_{i=1}^{n} R_i(t)
   \]  

b) **Parallel Structure**:  

   In a parallel system, the system works if at least one component functions.  
   
   Let \( R_i(t) \) be the return rate of the \( i \)-th component, where \( i = 1, 2, \ldots, n \).  
   
   The system response rate \( R_S(t) \) is the complement of the probability that all components fail:  
   
   \[
   R_S(t) = 1 - \prod_{i=1}^{n} (1 - R_i(t)) = 1 - \prod_{i=1}^{n} F_i(t)
   \]  
   
   The system failure rate \( F_S(t) \) is:  
   
   \[
   F_S(t) = 1 - R_S(t) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} (1 - R_i(t)) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} F_i(t)
   \]  

