C3H8 + 5O2 → 3CO2 + 4H2O

This equation shows that 1 mole of propane (C3H8) reacts to produce 3 moles of carbon dioxide (CO2) and 4 moles of hydrogen dioxide (H2O).

The standard heat of combustion of propane is given as -2220 kJ/mol. This means that 1 mole of propane reacts to release 2220 kJ of heat.

To find the total heat released when 2.5 moles of propane are boiled, we multiply the number of moles of propane by the heat of combustion per mole:

