To calculate the work done by the gas during the elastic expansion, we use the formula for the work done in an isothermal flow:

\[
W = -k \ln \left(\frac{V_f}{V_i}\right)
\]

where:
- \( W \) is the work done
- \( k \) is the gas constant per mole, which is \( k = 0.0923 \times 10^9 \, \text{J} \cdot \text{mol}^{-1} \)
- \( V_f \) is the final volume
- \( V_i \) is the initial volume

Given:
- \( V_i = 4 \, \text{L} \)
- \( V_f = 8 \, \text{L} \)
- \( n = 2 \, \text{mol} \)

The work done is:

\[
W = -n k \ln \left(\frac{V_f}{V_i}\right) = -2 \times 0.0923 \times 10^9 \, \text{J} \cdot \text{mol}^{-1} \ln \left(\frac{8 \, \text{L}}{4 \, \text{L}}\right)
\]

\[
W = -2 \times 0.0923 \times 10^9 \, \text{J} \cdot \text{mol}^{-1} \ln (2)
\]

\[
W = -0.1846 \times 10^9 \, \text{J} \times 0.6931
\]

\[
W = -128.6 \times 10^6 \, \text{J}
\]

The work done is \(\boxed{-128.6 \, \text{J}}\).
