","To find the percent change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \), we first need to find the initial and final closed-loop gains.

The open-loop voltage gain is \( A_v = 100 \). The feedback fraction is \( m_v = 0.1 \).

The transfer-loop gain is \( A_{tf} = A_v \times m_v = 100 \times 0.1 = 10 \).

The closed-loop voltage gain without feedback is given by:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - A_tv} = \frac{100}{1 - 100 \times 0.1} = \frac{100}{1 - 10} = \frac{100}{-9} = -11.11 \text{ V}
\]

The gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB, which means the new open-loop voltage gain \( A_v' \) is:
\[
A_{vf}' = A_v \times \frac{1}{2^6} = 100 \times \frac{1}{64} = 1.5625 \text{ V}
\]

The transfer-loop gain is now:
\[
